Docker Architecture: A Quick Breakdown for Devs & DevOps!
If you're diving into containers or brushing up on cloud-native fundamentals, understanding Docker's internal architecture is the key.

Here's a simple breakdown of Docker's core components:

🧩 1. Docker Client (CLI)
• docker command-line interface (e.g., docker run, docker build) sends commands to the Docker daemon via the REST API — typically over a Unix socket.

🧩 2. Docker REST API
•This is the same API the CLI uses under the hood. 
•It lets you programmatically control Docker: create containers, manage images, networks, and more.

🧩 3. Docker Daemon (dockerd)
•The brain of Docker, running in the background and responsible for:
Managing images, containers, volumes, and networks
•Communicating with other Docker daemons (e.g., in Swarm mode)
•Orchestrating everything from build to runtime

🧩 4. containerd
•A lightweight container runtime that handles:
•Container lifecycle: create, start, stop, delete Image pull and push
•Snapshot management. It’s also used by Kubernetes under the hood!

Key Points:
• The CLI talks to the daemon via REST API.
• The daemon delegates to lower-level runtimes like containerd and runc.
• All of this is transparent to the user, who only interacts with the CLI or Docker Desktop GUI.
